   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the banking law, in relation to electronic notices or\ndocuments in premium finance transactions",
    "basePrintNo": "S7369",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7369-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §578-a, Bank L",
    "lawSection": "Banking Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7369\n\nSPONSOR: SANDERS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the banking law, in relation to electronic notices or\ndocuments in premium finance transactions\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to allow the use of electronic commu-\nnications for requirednotices related to premium finance transactions\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\n§ 1 The banking law is amended by adding a new section 578-a - Electron-\nic notes and documents.\n\n§ 2 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2021 Governor Hochul signed into law Senate Bill S653A / Assembly\nBill A651A which allowed for electronic delivery of property and casual-\nty insurance notices. This bill seeks to similarly modernize the insur-\nance premium finance agency statute to allow for the option to use elec-\ntronic communications for required notices. Insurance premium financing\nis a short-term loan for businesses and individuals to specifically pay\nfor insurance coverage. An insurance premium finance loan enables the\ninsurance policy holder to spread payments over the course of the policy\nterm, instead of paying the entire premium up front as typically\nrequired by insurance carriers, Payment over time makes insurance more\naffordable for consumers and businesses and allows for other uses of\ncapital that would otherwise be paid to an insurer at the inception of\nan insurance policy period.  A significant proportion of consumer premi-\num finance loans in New York support consumers in more affordably\nobtaining and paying for required automobile insurance coverage.\n\nPremium finance companies would like to provide the option to their\ncustomers of consenting to receive all premium finance notices electron-\nically. Consumers and businesses currently use electronic communications\non a daily basis and often strongly prefer electronic notices to mailed\nnotices. Furthermore, New York has generally sought to have consistency\nin law for insurance and premium finance. Left unchanged, consumers may\nbelieve they are receiving an electronic notice before their policy\ncancels (because they opted into electronic notices with their insurer)\nand do not take note of a cancellation from a premium finance agency\nthat was physically mailed.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew legislation.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
